module generic_call.neg_2

// ONE seeded cause: two arguments want DIFFERENT types for one type parameter.
// `pick[T](a T, b T)` is called with an int and a str, so no single `T`
// satisfies both positions.
//
// Why this is a refusal and not a choice (E2-b3 wave 1): whichever argument
// "wins" decides which C symbol the call names. Picking the first one silently
// emits a call to `pick` instantiated at int while handing it a str -- and that
// is not a hypothetical. MEASURED 2026-08-23 on the same shape: the oracle
// PASSES it at `check` and clang then refuses the emission with "passing 'const
// nova_str' to parameter of incompatible type 'nova_int'". Seventh carrier of
// class O-1c, recorded in docs/dev/novac-divergences.md.
//
// SO THIS FIXTURE PINS NOVAC'S REFUSAL AND CLAIMS NO AGREEMENT: the oracle's
// outcome on this shape differs at check level, and a `neg_*` that pretended
// otherwise would assert a divergence as if it were a rule (the reason
// `dbl[Zork](21)` is not a fixture either -- see neg_1).
// The body returns an int, not `a`: returning a `ro`-bound parameter would add
// a SECOND cause (the oracle refuses that separately, `E_READONLY_COERCE`), and
// a fixture with two causes cannot say which one it pins.
fn pick[T](a T, b T) -> int => 2

fn main() {
    println(pick(1, "s"))
}
